      <p class="ai-summary-text">
        Not every camp labeled "luxury" in Merzouga delivers genuine comfort. A legitimate <strong>Merzouga luxury camp</strong> requires three non-negotiables: private en-suite ceramic flush toilets with high-pressure hot rainfall showers inside your tent, placement deep within the <strong>Erg Chebbi</strong> dune sea rather than beside the asphalt road, and silent solar battery power rather than noisy diesel generators humming outside your door all night.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <!-- Editorial Content -->
    <div class="article-content">
      <p style="font-size: 1.25rem;">
        Spending a night under the stars in the Sahara Desert is the highlight of most Moroccan trips. Yet travelers often arrive with two very different expectations. Some picture freezing in a damp nylon tent and stumbling across dark dunes to share a communal outhouse. Others expect a five-star hotel room miraculously dropped into a sand dune.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reality of modern Moroccan glamping sits somewhere in between. Over the past decade, high-end camp operators in <strong>Erg Chebbi</strong> (the towering orange dunes outside Merzouga) have solved the logistics of desert comfort. Today, you can sleep in a hand-crafted Berber tent with a king-sized mattress, plug your phone into a 220V wall outlet, take a steaming shower, and enjoy a slow-roasted tagine while local musicians play around a tamarisk wood fire.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, the word "luxury" is used loosely in Moroccan travel listings. Here is our honest, on-the-ground breakdown of what separates an authentic desert oasis from an overcrowded tourist bivouac.
      </p>

      <!-- Interactive Trip Cost Calculator Banner -->
      <div style="background: #faf8f5; border: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.08); border-left: 4px solid #C8A45C; border-radius: 16px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.03); padding: 24px; margin: 28px 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px;">
