      </div>

      <h2>The Anatomy of a True Luxury Tent</h2>
      <figure class="featured-image-box" style="margin: 32px 0;">
        <img src="/images/pol-tent.jpg" alt="Interior of a luxury desert camp tent with comfortable double bed and Moroccan handwoven rugs" width="1200" height="675">
        <figcaption>Interior comfort: Real wooden beds, hand-woven tribal rugs, and solid flooring.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        If you are paying for luxury glamping, you should not be "roughing it." Here are the exact architectural and amenity standards you should expect:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Private En-Suite Bathroom:</strong> The tent interior is divided by an insulated partition wall. Behind it sits a private washroom equipped with a modern ceramic flush toilet, a stone vanity basin with running tap water, and a separate walk-in shower stall with reliable hot water.</li>
        <li><strong>Solid Raised Flooring:</strong> Cheaper camps lay carpets directly on loose sand, which means every step kicks up fine dust into your bedsheets. True luxury camps build wood or composite decking platforms under every tent, covered with authentic Beni Ourain and Boucherouite wool rugs.</li>
        <li><strong>Heavy-Duty Weather Canvas:</strong> Authentic desert tents are constructed with multi-layered canvas lined with dense camel-hair or sheep-wool felt. This insulation keeps the tent cooler during blazing afternoon sun and retains heat during chilly desert nights.</li>
        <li><strong>Solar Electricity (220V Outlets):</strong> You will have real reading lamps, ceiling illumination, and standard Moroccan two-pin electrical outlets to charge cameras, phones, and drone batteries without relying on noisy combustion engines.</li>
        <li><strong>Quality Bedding:</strong> Expect proper hotel-grade mattresses with high-thread-count Egyptian cotton sheets, fluffy pillows, and heavy wool blankets.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Deep Dunes vs. Roadside Camps: The Location Trap</h2>
      <p>
        This is the single biggest trap in Merzouga tourism. Online booking platforms show identical photos of sand dunes, but they rarely disclose where the camp is actually situated.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Perimeter (Roadside) Camps:</strong> Dozens of low-cost camps sit only 200 to 400 meters from the paved N13 highway. While they have sand behind them, you will hear truck traffic throughout the night, see headlights sweeping across your tent canvas, and smell exhaust from rental quad bikes tearing across the flats.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Deep Dune Camps:</strong> Legitimate luxury camps are nestled deep inside the <strong>Erg Chebbi</strong> dune sea, often a 20 to 30-minute 4x4 drive off-road from the nearest village (Hassilabied or Merzouga). High 150-meter sand walls surround the camp on all sides, blocking artificial light and road noise entirely. When you step outside your tent at midnight, you experience absolute silence and a Bortle Class 1 dark sky with the Milky Way visible to the naked eye.
      </p>

