      <h2>A Typical Evening at Camp: What to Expect</h2>
      <figure class="featured-image-box" style="margin: 32px 0;">
        <img src="/images/pol-twilight.jpg" alt="Warm twilight glow over desert camp with lanterns and glowing campfire" width="1200" height="675">
        <figcaption>Twilight in the Sahara: Lantern-lit walkways and the central campfire.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Your desert experience usually begins around 4:30 PM. After a scenic journey through the Atlas Mountains with your <a href="/morocco-private-driver.html"><strong>private driver</strong></a>, you arrive at a base riad in Merzouga to freshen up and leave heavy suitcases.
      </p>

      <p>
        From here, you mount your camel for a peaceful trek into Erg Chebbi. As the sun sinks below the horizon, the sand changes from bright mustard yellow to deep rust red. You arrive at camp just as the dusk lanterns are lit along the sand paths.
      </p>

      <p>
        After settling into your tent, dinner is served in a decorated restaurant tent. A traditional luxury desert meal includes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Warm bowls of spiced Moroccan <em>harira</em> soup with dates and sweet chebakia pastries.</li>
        <li>Fresh Moroccan salads with diced tomatoes, cucumbers, roasted peppers, and cumin.</li>
        <li>Slow-cooked beef or lamb tagine with caramelised prunes, toasted almonds, and sesame seeds, served with freshly baked khobz bread.</li>
        <li>Dessert of fresh seasonal melons or orange slices dusted with cinnamon.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        After dinner, guests gather around a central tamarisk campfire. Camp staff, who are native Amazigh men from the surrounding valley, play traditional hand drums (derboukas) and sing desert nomadic songs. Many guests lay blankets on the sand dunes away from the fire to gaze at the stars before retiring to their heated tents.
      </p>

      <h2>Seasonal Realities: Weather, Freezes &amp; Furnace Heat</h2>
      <p>
        The Sahara Desert has an extreme climate. Understanding the seasons is critical before booking:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spring (March to May):</strong> The most popular season. Daytime temperatures average 25°C to 30°C (77°F to 86°F), and evenings are comfortably mild (14°C to 18°C). Occasional spring dust storms can occur, making a cotton <em>cheche</em> (turban) essential.</li>
        <li><strong>Autumn (September to November):</strong> Excellent travel window. Warm days and comfortable nights with very low wind. The date harvest in the nearby Tafilalet oasis is in full swing.</li>
        <li><strong>Winter (December to February):</strong> Clear blue skies during the day with temperatures around 18°C to 20°C (65°F to 68°F). However, nighttime temperatures drop rapidly once the sun sets, frequently reaching 2°C to 5°C (35°F to 41°F). If you travel in winter, a camp with electric heaters and thick blankets is mandatory.</li>
        <li><strong>Summer (July and August):</strong> Extreme heat. Daytime temperatures regularly exceed 42°C to 46°C (108°F to 115°F). We advise travelers to avoid desert camping during these two months, opting instead for mountain trekking in the High Atlas or coastal stays in Essaouira.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        For a month-by-month temperature breakdown, check our guide on the <a href="/blog/best-time-to-visit-sahara-morocco.html"><strong>best time to visit the Sahara</strong></a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What to Pack for an Overnight in Erg Chebbi</h2>
      <p>
        Keep your packing for the camp contained in one small backpack or weekend duffel. Your main hard-shell suitcases remain secured in your private driver's vehicle or at the staging riad.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cotton Tuareg Cheche (Turban):</strong> A 4-meter cotton scarf wrapped around your head and face protects your eyes, nose, and mouth from windblown sand far better than any baseball cap. Your driver or camel guide can help you tie it properly.</li>
        <li><strong>Sunglasses with UV400 Protection:</strong> The glare from white-gold sand under desert sun is intense. Wrap-around sunglasses prevent sand from blowing in behind the lenses.</li>
        <li><strong>Warm Evening Layer:</strong> Even during warm months, desert breezes feel brisk after dark. Pack a fleece, wool pullover, or light down jacket.</li>
        <li><strong>Slip-on Footwear:</strong> Running shoes fill with sand within minutes. Bring slip-on sandals or canvas shoes that you can easily shake out before stepping into your tent.</li>
        <li><strong>Eye Drops &amp; Moisturizer:</strong> Sahara air is exceptionally dry. Saline eye drops and lip balm prevent irritation after hours in the wind.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Transport and Circuit Integration -->
      <h2>Connecting Merzouga to Your Morocco Itinerary</h2>
      <p>
        Merzouga is located in southeastern Morocco, roughly 560 kilometers from Marrakech and 460 kilometers from Fes. Because the drive crosses mountain ranges, you should never attempt to drive directly from Marrakech to the Sahara in a single day.
      </p>
      <p>
        The most enjoyable way to experience the desert is as part of a multi-day point-to-point journey. Our <a href="/blog/morocco-7-day-itinerary.html"><strong>7-day Morocco itinerary</strong></a> breaks the crossing with an overnight stay in the dramatic Dades Gorge, allowing you to visit UNESCO Ait Benhaddou and Todra Gorge along the way. If you have more time, our <a href="/blog/morocco-10-day-itinerary.html"><strong>10-day Grand Circuit</strong></a> adds the imperial history of Rabat and Casablanca.
      </p>

